rk applies to adapting R&D and compliance. Probably solves identified problem directly related. Could be 4 = solves identified problem directly related. Or 3 = solves implied problem. Hmm.\n\nThe proposal is about documentation beyond minimum, not logs. Might be applicability 3? It addresses record-keeping broadly, which is related. I'd say applicability 4: solves identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (compliance). The mandate includes technical documentation per recital 71. So yes.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving\n\nThis proposal turns regulatory documentation into reproducible-science asset and thought leadership. Changes relationship between R&D and compliance obligations - makes compliance a scientific output. That is paradigm stretching? Might be paradigm stretching: changes that relationship. Paradigm breaking requires introduces new elements AND changes relationship ... or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. This might be paradigm breaking? It introduces open science framework, changes relationship. Could be 3 or 4.\n\nThe description: paradigm breaking introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, OR addresses larger problem of what the lab researches. It does change relationship. Does it introduce new elements? Yes template/tooling. Probably qualifies as paradigm breaking? Hmm maybe paradigm stretching is enough: logging woven into science. This seems like paradigm stretching rather than breaking. Let's pick 3.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious, imaginative, surprising\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common\n\nOpen comprehensibility documentation framework beyond minimum... publishing template. Is this common? Many labs publish documentation standards. Might be unusual interesting. Probably 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\nThis is open science, transparency, exceeds compliance. Acceptable. 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, expensive or difficult\nDeveloping template and tooling, publishing public repository. That's some effort but reasonably feasible. Might be 3.\n\nLet's output.\n\nNeed format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nOrder: effectiveness, applicability, paradigm_relatedness, originality, acceptability, implementability\n\nProvide JSON? Probably lines.\n\nI'll produce:\n\nid: P1342\neffectiveness_evidence: ...\neffectiveness: 3\n...\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft concise sentences.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: It creates open documentation tooling that supports traceability and post-market monitoring but does not deliver the automatic event logging required by Article 12(1)-(3) for high-risk systems.\n\nApplicability evidence: The framework directly addresses the lab's compliance obligation to maintain clear, comprehensive records under Recital 71 and adapt R&D practices to regulatory requirements.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: It reframes compliance documentation as an open-science research output, changing how R&D and compliance obligations relate rather than treating logging as a separate administrative task.\n\nOriginality evidence: Publishing a lightweight metadata schema with automated generation scripts for model provenance is unusual for labs facing Article 12 and shows imaginative reuse of compliance work.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Openly sharing documentation templates and tools for AI transparency aligns with scientific norms and poses no legal or political objection.\n\nImplementability evidence: Building and maintaining a public template, schema, and automation scripts requires dedicated engineering and ongoing curation beyond routine logging.\n\nScores: effectiveness 3, applicability 4, paradigm_relatedness 3, originality 3, acceptability 4, implementability 3\n\nCheck paradigm_relatedness maybe 4? Let's keep 3.\n\nProvide final output.",
