ionship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\nSlightly = introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice\nPreserving = standard compliance.\n\nClassification gate is essentially a process change weaving compliance earlier. It changes relationship: R&D gated by compliance classification. Might be paradigm stretching level 3. Not breaking because doesn't address larger problem of what lab researches, just gating.\n\nSo paradigm_relatedness = 3? Could argue 2. Hmm \"introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\". The gate is a process tool. Might be slightly stretching.\n\nLet's think: experienced evaluator expects labs to give responses like \"implement logging\", \"hire compliance officer\". A formal decision point at PoC with checklist and sign-off before compute/data access is more than standard. It changes relationship between R&D and compliance obligations – compliance becomes gatekeeper for resources. That's paradigm stretching. I'll pick 3.\n\noriginality (1-4): degree idea is rare but ingenious...\nIs classification gate unusual, interesting; shows some imagination? Or common mundane? In regulated industries, stage-gate compliance review is fairly common. For AI labs maybe not common but not surprising. Probably interesting = 2. Could be unusual interesting =3. The proposal is a two-page checklist and sign-off before compute. Might be somewhat uncommon but not ingenious. I'd say 2 = interesting. Maybe 3? Let's pick 3? Hmm.\n\nCommon practice in pharma is regulatory gate. For AI lab maybe novel. \"unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\" fits. I'll give 3.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented.\nEasy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes =4\nSome changes, or reasonably feasible measures =3\nSignificant change... =2\nTotally infeasible =1\n\nDraft two-page checklist, require leads complete and route through sign-off before accessing compute. That's easy low cost. So 4.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies that violate no norms =4\nSomewhat uncommon but do not offend =3\nOffends sensibilities somewhat =2\nRadically violates =1\n\nGate with legal sign-off is acceptable. Common. 4.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree will solve problem.\nReasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability =4\nReasonable and will contribute to solution =3\nUnreasonable or unlikely =2\nSolves unrelated problem=1\n\nProblem: must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Proposal prevents spending on prototypes that later fail to meet logging requirements, protects budget/reputation. Does it meet logging mandate? It ensures classification determines which logging fields mandated before investment, so helps avoid non-compliance but does not actually implement logging. It contributes to solution, partial. Effectiveness maybe 3 (contribute). Could be 4? It will solve problem of spending resources inefficiently, but stated problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Classification gate adapts R&D accordingly, so it solves adaptation part. Might be reasonable and will solve stated problem? It doesn't build logs, just decides what logs needed. The mandate requires technical logging capabilities. Gate alone doesn't provide logs. So partial. 3.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree clearly applies to stated problem.\nSolves identified problem directly related =4\nSolves implied problem related =3\nMay have some benefit within special situation... =2\nIntervention not stated...=1\n\nIt directly addresses deciding how to meet logging mandate before R&D investment. That's directly related. So 4.\n\nReturn rating with evidence sentence then score.\n\nNeed format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence first then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably output lines.\n\nLet's prod