ute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial solution). Could argue 4? It says satisfying Article12(3)(b)-(d) without ad-hoc engineering. The mandate also includes 1 and 2. But for biometric prototypes, 12(3) is minimum. Missing period. Hmm.\n\nMaybe effectiveness 3.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged\n\nProposal is reusable architectural pattern coupling prototype to metadata, interface contract forces registration... validate conformance through automated architecture-review scripts. Is this slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Might be slightly paradigm stretching: introduces pattern and CI enforcement, but compliance remains logging practice. Not changing relationship fundamentally? Could be paradigm stretching because logging woven into science via interface contract forces each prototype to register before returning result – logging woven into science. That is change relationship. Possibly 3.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is rare but ingenious.\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane\n\nReusable architectural pattern for traceability with immutable query-time metadata, interface contract enforced via CI. That's fairly standard software engineering practice. Probably interesting but not rare. Might be 2 = interesting. Could be 3? Reference-database traceability pattern... Hmm experienced evaluator would expect labs to give response like \"build logging\". This is more specific. Maybe unusual interesting shows some imagination -> 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies violate no norms\nProposal is compliant, no violation. So 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive/difficult\n\nDevelop reusable architectural pattern, define interface contract in design system, force prototypes to register, validate conformance through automated architecture-review scripts in CI. That's some changes, reasonably feasible. Not trivial but doable. Might be 3. Could be 4 easy? Might require redesign of prototypes. Probably 3.\n\nLet's output.\n\nNeed format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nOrder: applicability, effectiveness, paradigm_relatedness, originality, acceptability, implementability\n\nProvide sentences.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft.\n\napplicability_evidence: The proposal directly addresses meeting Article 12 logging requirements for Annex III 1(a) biometric prototypes by embedding traceability metadata into R&D.\napplicability:4\n\neffectiveness_evidence: It provides immutable query-time metadata for reference database, matched input and verifier identity, covering three of four minimum logging items for biometric systems and enabling repeatable compliance.\neffectiveness:3 (partial because period of use not covered)\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: It weaves logging into the design system via an enforced interface contract and CI checks, changing how R&D prototypes are built rather than adding logging afterwards.\nparadigm_relatedness:3\n\noriginality