andate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Proposal measures overhead of logging-first vs retrofit to decide resourcing. Solves identified problem directly related? It provides numbers to decide implementation approach. That is directly related to meeting mandate. So applicability 4 = solves identified problem directly related.\n\neffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem. It will provide data to decide, but does not itself implement logging. Reasonable and will contribute to solution, partial solution. Effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (it helps but only partial). Could be 3.\n\nCheck anchors: effectiveness 4 = will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). Does benchmark solve logging mandate? No, just informs decision. So 3.\n\nThus output.\n\nNeed format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence one sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON? Instruction says return rating... Provide lines.\n\nI'll output:\n\nid: P2591\noriginality_evidence: ...\noriginality: 2\n...\n\nLet's decide originality score. The idea of prototyping overhead benchmark is interesting but common A/B engineering comparison. I'd say 2 = interesting. Could be 3 unusual. Hmm reference class: experienced evaluator expects labs to give responses like implement logging, hire compliance officer... A controlled internal experiment measuring extra engineering time is somewhat unusual, shows some imagination. Might be 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice => 2\n\nimplementability: significant change expensive => 2\n\nacceptability: 4\n\napplicability: 4\n\neffectiveness: 3\n\nLet's give originality 3? Let's pick 2 to be safe: interesting not unusual.\n\nI'll choose originality 2, paradigm_relatedness 2, implementability 2, acceptability 4, applicability 4, effectiveness 3.\n\nProvide one sentence evidence each.\n\n",
