 politically acceptable. Common strategies violate no norms. That's common compliance governance. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: Assigning protected steward time and quarterly reviews are standard, non-offensive compliance management practices.\n\nScore 4.\n\nimplementability: easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. Some changes, reasonably feasible measures? Protected ten percent time for engineer per lab may be scarce resource cost. That's some changes, reasonably feasible. Could be 3. Not easy low cost? Might be 3.\n\nEvidence: Requires reallocating existing engineering capacity and establishing recurring meetings, which is feasible but not trivial in a resource-constrained R&D lab.\n\nScore 3.\n\nparadigm_relatedness: degree preserves/modifies paradigm anchored to problem. Proposal introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. Slightly paradigm stretching? Introduces documentation steward role, lifecycle governance. Still treats compliance as logging practice. Might be 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice.\n\nEvidence: It adds stewardship and rolling reviews to existing R&D workflows without changing the fundamental relationship between research and compliance obligations.\n\nScore 2.\n\noriginality: degree rare but ingenious. Common mundane boring? Rolling documentation governance is common. Maybe 1 = common, mundane, boring. Could be 2 interesting? I'd say common. Score 1.\n\nEvidence: Quarterly documentation reviews with a designated steward are a widely used, routine compliance management approach in regulated labs.\n\nScore 1.\n\nReturn JSON? Probably format as lines. Instructions: For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nEcho id exactly.\n\nLet's output:\n\nid: P1027\neffectiveness_evidence: ...\neffectiveness: 3\n...\n\nEnsure order listed: effectiveness, applicability, acceptabil