ocess improvement, fully acceptable. 4.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id. Likely provide lines.\n\nWe'll produce:\n\nid: P1817\neffectiveness_evidence: ...\neffectiveness: 3\netc.\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence each.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: The templates embed logging hooks and documentation stubs matching Annex III so prototypes begin structurally compliant, directly reducing setup effort for Article 12 record-keeping. Score 3? Wait sentence should reflect reasoning before number. Might say contributes but not guarantee full compliance.\n\nBetter: It will contribute to meeting the logging mandate by front-loading hooks and stubs, but it does not by itself ensure correct content or ongoing maintenance required by Article 12. That's one sentence. Score 3.\n\nApplicability evidence: The proposal directly targets how the lab meets the automatic logging and traceability requirements for high-risk AI systems under Article 12. Score 4.\n\nOriginality evidence: Pre-wired compliance scaffolds per risk class are a familiar DevOps templating practice applied to AI Act logging, making it interesting but not ingenious or surprising. Score 2.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: It introduces reusable templates and registry processes while still treating compliance as a logging practice rather than changing the lab's R&D-compliance relationship. Score 2.\n\nImplementability evidence: Auditing four recent prototypes and codifying three tiered templates into a shared registry requires modest engineering effort and is reasonably feasible with existing CI tooling. Score 3.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Standardizing internal project scaffolds for regulatory documentation is a common, norm-conforming operational improvement with no legal or sensibility concerns. Score 4.\n\nReturn.\n\n",
