egies that do not offend sensibilities.\n\nEvidence: Using differential privacy and federated computation for compliance logging is not standard practice but does not violate legal norms or sensibilities.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented.\nEasy to implement at low cost? No, research programme exploring cryptographic and statistical privacy mechanisms, prototype pipeline, measure utility-privacy trade-off. That's significant change, expensive/difficult but not totally impossible. So 2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible.\n\nEvidence: Building a prototype pipeline with cryptographic privacy mechanisms and auditing against regulatory requirements requires substantial R&D effort and specialized expertise.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving\n\nProposal opens genuinely new scientific frontier at intersection of formal privacy, ML systems engineering, and compliance law. That sounds like paradigm breaking? It changes relationship between R&D and compliance obligations - making compliance a research frontier. Might be paradigm stretching? It introduces new elements and changes relationship. The description says opens genuinely new scientific frontier... with broad impact beyond institution. Might be paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches.\n\nProbably 4