 solve stated problem without regard for workability? Will standardising logs help meet Article12? Yes. Will repurposing help adapt R&D? It contributes. Might be partial solution? The main problem is meet logging mandate. This does that. Could be 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem. Maybe 3 contribute? It solves logging mandate via standardization, and also adds scientific value. I'd say 4.\n\nOriginality: degree not only rare but ingenious. Not expressed before AND ingenious... Is repurposing compliance logs as scientific instrument rare and ingenious? Unusual interesting shows some imagination = 3. Maybe 4? Could be common idea to reuse data. I think unusual interesting, shows some imagination = 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree preserves or modifies paradigm. 4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. This turns compliance artifact into empirical resource, changes relationship. Could be paradigm stretching? Changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science). That's definition of 3: changes that relationship. Paradigm breaking requires introduces new elements AND changes relationship... Or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. Does it address larger problem? It studies real-world generalisation beyond benchmarks. Maybe paradigm breaking. The description: paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes the relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses the larger problem of what the lab researches. This does introduce new elements (common schema, pipelines) and changes relationship. Could be 4. Also \"irreplaceable for studying real-world generalisation beyond benchmarks\" suggests addresses larger problem. Might be 4.\n\nLet's choose paradigm_relatedness 3? Hmm. The anchor: 4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes the relationship... or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. This seems to address larger problem. So 4.\n\nLet's output evidence sentence then score.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale in order listed: FIRST write one sentence of evidence for that subscale (reasoning before number). THEN give its score as integer level.\n\nProbably output lines:\n\nimplementability_evidence: ...\nimplementability: 3\n\netc.\n\nEnsure id echoed.\n\nLet's produce.\n\nImplementability evidence: Standardising log schema across deployed high-risk systems and building mining pipelines requires coordinated engineering and data-science work but uses existing mandated logs. => score 3\n\nAcceptability evidence: Repurposing legally required logs for internal research does not violate EU AI Act norms or business sensibilities. => 4\n\nApplicability evidence: The proposal directly addresses the mandate to produce Article 12 logs by standardising them and adapts R&D by using them scientifically. => 4\n\nEffectiveness evidence: If implemented, common schema logging satisfies traceability and post-market monitoring requirements while creating a longitudinal behavioural dataset. => 4\n\nOriginality evidence: Turning a compliance artifact into a unique longitudinal scientific resource is unusual and shows imagination beyond routine logging. => 3\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: Logging is woven into the scientific workflow and changes how compliance obligations shape research, moving beyond standard compliance practice. => 3 or 4. Let's pick 4? Hmm.\n\nDefinition of paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into the science, new partners or offerings born from compliance). Paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship... or addresses larger problem of what lab researches.\n\nIt does both. I'd say paradigm breaking =4.\n\nProvide evidence sentence accordingly.\n\nLet's output.\n\n",
