e stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem)\n3 = reasonable and will contribute...\nDoes reference codebase solve logging mandate for the lab? It demonstrates architecture, but lab still needs to implement in its own system. Reference may help but does not automatically ensure lab's own systems log correctly. Might be partial solution: provides blueprint but not actual logs. Could be considered contributes to solution, not fully solve. The lab must meet logging mandate for its own systems; providing open source reference doesn't guarantee compliance for its products unless it adopts it. It could be used internally. Arguably if you could do it, it would provide a compliant logging solution they can use. Hmm.\n\nInterpretation: Effectiveness about solving stated problem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Building reference codebase that meets requirements would give them a way to meet mandate. If they adopt it, yes solves. But proposal is to release publicly, allocate team to build and maintain repository. Might be more about market differentiation than internal compliance.\n\nProbably effectiveness =3 reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Because it provides reference but still need integration. Or could be 4 if you assume they use it internally.\n\nLet's pick 3: contributes.\n\nEvidence: A reference implementation can guide compliant logging but does not by itself guarantee the lab’s own high-risk systems are logging as required.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\nOpen source compliance reference is acceptable. No violation. So 4.\n\nEvidence: Publishing an open-source logging reference for regulatory compliance is a common, non-controversial practice that violates no legal or professional norms.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change...\nTeam of two engineers + one compliance analyst for 18 months is moderate cost but feasible. Probably not easy low cost. That's some changes, reasonably feasible. So 3.\n\nEvidence: Maintaining a dedicated three-person team for eighteen months requires meaningful staffing and sustained effort, though it is financially feasible for an R&D lab.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is rare but ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n1 common mundane\nOpen source reference implementation for AI Act compliance is somewhat uncommon but not surprising. Probably 3? Might be 2 interesting. The instrument: originality anchored to rarity in reference class of responses experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give to this mandate.\n\nExpected responses: implement logging internally, buy vendor solution, hire consultant. Open source reference is unusual, interesting. Might be 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination.\n\nEvidence: Releasing a public open-source compliance reference for biometric logging is unusual among labs focused on internal compliance and shows imaginative market positioning.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving\n\nProposal positions regulatory burden into market differentiation and customer trust. That's changing relationship: compliance becomes offering. Introduces new elements (open source). Probably paradigm stretching: changes that relationship, new partners/offerings born from compliance. So 3.\n\nEvidence: Turning mandatory logging into a public reference and market positioning reframes compliance from internal burden to external authority offering.\n\nThus scores:\napplicability 4\neffectiveness 3\nacceptability 4\nimplementability 3\noriginality 3\nparadigm_relatedness 3\n\nNeed format: echo id exactly. For each subscale in order listed, give \"<subscale>_evidence\" (one sentence) first and then \"<subscale>\" (integer).\n\nProbably output JSON? Not specified. Likely list.\n\nProvide:\n\nid P1659\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability:4\n...\n\nReturn rating.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence each.\n\nLet's produce clean output.\n\n",
