om compliance. That's exactly. So 3.\n\nEvidence sentence: It reframes mandatory post-market logs as an active research input, weaving compliance data collection into the lab's scientific work.\n\nScore 3.\n\noriginality (1-4): degree rare and ingenious.\nIs it unusual, interesting; shows some imagination? 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious... Might be common idea to reuse data. Probably not that rare. I'd say 2 = interesting? Or 3? Let's think: Reusing compliance logs for research is plausible but maybe common. The taxonomy tagging and quarterly queries is somewhat imaginative. I'd rate 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination.\n\nEvidence: The proposal recasts a regulatory burden as a free scientific corpus with systematic tagging, which is an imaginative repurposing rather than a common compliance tactic.\n\nScore 3.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\nProblem: meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce resources.\nWill this solve logging mandate? It uses storage already provisioned for log infrastructure, tags events. It does not describe actually implementing logs to comply with Article 12 requirements (recording period of use, reference database, etc.). It focuses on reusing post-market monitoring data streams. Might contribute to solution? It assumes logs exist. It doesn't ensure compliance logging capabilities per Article 12(3). So effectiveness maybe partial: contributes to solution by making compliance more valuable, but does not guarantee meeting mandate. Definition: 4 = will solve stated problem without regard for workability. 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (helps, but partial). Idea converts obligation into data, justifying funding, but does not itself implement logging. Might be partial.\n\nCould be 3: it contributes by increasing incentives to maintain logs properly, but does not directly ensure technical