ore 4.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented.\nReuses storage already provisioned, compliance engineering micro-team tags, ML group queries quarterly. Some changes but reasonably feasible. That's 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Could be 4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. Tagging each logged event with taxonomy may require effort. Probably some changes.\n\nEvidence: It requires only tagging and quarterly querying on top of existing log infrastructure, which is reasonably feasible.\n\nScore 3.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree socially, legally, politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies that violate no norms? Using logs for research. Might need consent/privacy concerns for logs containing input data, identification of natural persons per Article 12(3)(d). Using logs as research corpus may raise privacy/legal issues. But proposal mentions risk categories. Could be somewhat uncommon but not offend sensibilities. Acceptability likely 3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities. Or 4? Probably 3.\n\nEvidence: Reusing compliance data for internal research is unusual but does not inherently violate legal norms if handled appropriately.\n\nScore 3.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence (one sentence) first then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON? It says return rating echoing its id. Provide entries.\n\nLet's output lines:\n\nid: P1236\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 3\n...\n\nMake sure order listed.\n\nProvide six subscales in order: paradigm_relatedness, originality, effectiveness, applicability, implementability, acceptability.\n\nDo one sentence evidence each.\n\nProceed.",
