ion is fairly common/mundane. Probably 1 or 2. Might be common practice in IT. I'd say common, mundane, boring => 1.\n\nImplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive/difficult but not totally impossible\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nBuilding internal financial model, survey log volumes, deliver calculator. Seems reasonably feasible, some changes. Could be 3? Easy to implement at low cost? Might need analyst time. I'd say 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible.\n\nAcceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat...\n1 = radically violates...\n\nBudget modeling is acceptable. Common strategies. =>4\n\nApplicability (1-4):\n4 = solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, Y part of stated problem)\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation and somehow relates\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nStated problem: lab must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore.\n\nCost model does it help meet logging mandate? It addresses budgeting for logs, but doesn't actually implement logging. It addresses financial planning for compliance, which is related but not directly meeting logging mandate. Might be implied problem: budget overrun. The stated problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Cost model helps adapt R&D funding process to account for logging costs. Is that applicability 4? Solves identified problem directly related? Problem is meet logging mandate. Cost model doesn't create logs. It makes budgeting transparent. Could be solves implied problem of resource allocation. Might be 3 = solves implied problem related.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4):\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (helps but partial)\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nWill cost model solve logging mandate? No. It helps avoid budget break, contributes partially to compliance by ensuring funding exists, but does not implement logs. So contributes to solution => 3? Might be 3.\n\nAlternatively effectiveness could be 2 because it doesn't actually provide logs. Hmm.\n\nThe instrument: effectiveness degree to which idea will solve problem. Problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Cost model adapts R&D funding process, so does contribute. I'd give 3.\n\nLet's output JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably JSON object with fields.\n\nWe need first write one sentence of evidence for that subscale (your reasoning before the number). Then give its score as integer.\n\nProbably format:\n\nid: P1307\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: \"...\"\nparadigm_relatedness: 2\n...\n\nProvide in order listed.\n\nLet's produce clean output.\n\nEvidence sentences must be one sentence each.\n\nParadigm relatedness evidence: The model adds a budgeting tool but keeps compliance as a logging cost to be funded rather than changing how R&D and compliance interact. Score 2.\n\nOriginality evidence: Estimating storage costs for regulated logs is a routine financial planning practice commonly used by labs. Score 1.\n\nImplementability evidence: Building a calculator from existing log volume data and analyst time requires modest organizational change and is reasonably feasible. Score 3.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Internal cost modeling and budget scrutiny are standard, norm-compliant management practices. Score 4.\n\nApplicability evidence: The proposal addresses the implied resource-allocation problem of 